PAUSE 900
SAY   Function Annotations.
PAUSE 400
SAY   With the acceptance of PEP 484, the style rules for function annotations have changed.
PAUSE 500
SAY   Function annotations should use PEP 484 syntax (there are some formatting recommendations for annotations in the previous section).
PAUSE 300
SAY   The experimentation with annotation styles that was recommended previously in this PEP is no longer encouraged.
PAUSE 300
SAY   However, outside the stdlib, experiments within the rules of PEP 484 are now encouraged. For example, marking up a large third party library or application with PEP 484 style type annotations, reviewing how easy it was to add those annotations, and observing whether their presence increases code understandability.
PAUSE 300
SAY   The Python standard library should be conservative in adopting such annotations, but their use is allowed for new code and for big refactorings.
PAUSE 300
SAY   For code that wants to make a different use of function annotations it is recommended to put a comment of the form: # type: ignore near the top of the file; this tells type checkers to ignore all annotations. (More fine-grained ways of disabling complaints from type checkers can be found in PEP 484.).
PAUSE 300
SAY   Like linters, type checkers are optional, separate tools. Python interpreters by default should not issue any messages due to type checking and should not alter their behavior based on annotations.
PAUSE 300
SAY   Users who don't want to use type checkers are free to ignore them. However, it is expected that users of third party library packages may want to run type checkers over those packages. For this purpose PEP 484 recommends the use of stub files:.pyi files that are read by the type checker in preference of the corresponding.py files. Stub files can be distributed with a library, or separately (with the library author's permission) through the typeshed repo.
PAUSE 500
SAY   Code omitted.
PAUSE 900
SAY   Variable Annotations.
PAUSE 400
SAY   PEP 526 introduced variable annotations. The style recommendations for them are similar to those on function annotations described above:
PAUSE 500
SAY   Annotations for module level variables, class and instance variables, and local variables should have a single space after the colon.
PAUSE 300
SAY   There should be no space before the colon.
PAUSE 300
SAY   If an assignment has a right hand side, then the equality sign should have exactly one space on both sides: # Correct: code: int class Point: coords: Tuple[int, int] label: str = '<unknown>' # Wrong: code:int # No space after colon code: int # Space before colon class Test: result: int=0 # No spaces around equality sign.
PAUSE 300
SAY   Although the PEP 526 is accepted for Python 3.6, the variable annotation syntax is the preferred syntax for stub files on all versions of Python (see PEP 484 for details).
PAUSE 500
